exercises.instructions:
Events let your page respond to user actions. `element.addEventListener(type, handler)` attaches a function that runs when the event occurs.

Syntax:
```
const button = document.getElementById("myBtn");
button.addEventListener("click", () => {
  // runs every time the button is clicked
});
```

The first argument is the event type (a string like `"click"`, `"submit"`, `"keydown"`). The second is a function that runs when that event fires.

Write `setupCounter(buttonId, displayId)` — find the button and display elements by ID. Initialize the display to "0". Each click should increment the count and update the display (see `@param` in the starter code).

exercises.starterCode:
/**
 * @param {string} buttonId - ID of the button to listen on
 * @param {string} displayId - ID of the element to show the count
 * Sets display to "0" initially, increments by 1 on each button click.
 */
function setupCounter(buttonId, displayId) {

}

exercises.solution:
function setupCounter(buttonId, displayId) {
  const button = document.getElementById(buttonId);
  const display = document.getElementById(displayId);
  let count = 0;
  display.textContent = String(count);
  button.addEventListener("click", () => {
    count++;
    display.textContent = String(count);
  });
}

