exercises.instructions:
The `async` keyword before a function makes it always return a Promise. Inside an `async` function, you can use `await` to pause execution until a Promise settles.

```
async function getData() {
  const result = await somePromise;
  return result; // automatically wrapped in a Promise
}
```

`await` unwraps the resolved value from a Promise so you can work with it directly — no `.then()` callbacks needed. If the awaited Promise rejects, `await` throws the rejection reason as an error.

Write `async function getUpperCase(promise)` — await the promise (which resolves to a string), convert it to upper case, and return it.

